## Runtime settings

Set these as environment variables on the function:

- `POWERSHELL_RUNTIME_VERBOSE=TRUE` writes the runtime's own progress (each step of start-up and of
  every invocation) to the function log. It does not turn on `Write-Verbose` output from the handler or
  the modules it uses: set `$VerbosePreference` in the handler for that.
- `POWERSHELL_RUNTIME_JSON_DEPTH` (1-100, default 100) is the depth to which the handler's output is
  serialized to JSON; any other value uses 100, and the runtime writes a warning naming the value to
  the function log once. Return plain data (hashtables, PSCustomObjects, arrays, strings, numbers): a
  rich .NET object, such as the output of `Get-Item`, references other objects without end, and
  serializing it that deep can exhaust the function's memory or time. Lower the depth if a handler must
  return one; values nested deeper than the depth are sent as their type name (for example
  `System.Collections.Hashtable`).

Everything the handler writes to the pipeline is its response. A string is sent as it is; anything else
is sent as JSON. PowerShell unrolls a one-item array, so return `, @($item)` to send a JSON array of
one, and discard stray output (for example `$null = $list.Add($x)`), which would otherwise be sent as
part of an array. A handler that writes nothing to the pipeline returns an empty body.

`Write-Host` and `Write-Warning` output goes to the function log; `Write-Information` and
`Write-Verbose` output goes there only when the handler sets `$InformationPreference` or
`$VerbosePreference`. `Write-Error` does not fail the invocation: the handler goes on and its output is
the response. A `throw`, or a command run with `-ErrorAction Stop` that fails, fails the invocation and
reports the error. `$LambdaContext` has `AwsRequestId`, `ClientContext`, `FunctionName`,
`FunctionVersion`, `Identity`, `InvokedFunctionArn`, `LogGroupName`, `LogStreamName`,
`MemoryLimitInMB`, `RemainingTime` and `GetRemainingTimeInMillis()`.

A handler receives the event and the context as its first two arguments, so start it with
`param($LambdaInput, $LambdaContext)`: without that block, `$LambdaInput` and `$LambdaContext` are
`$null`. The handler setting (`_HANDLER`) takes one of three forms: `<script>.ps1`,
`<script>.ps1::<function>` or `Module::<module>::<function>`. If the handler cannot be loaded, the
function fails at start-up and the invoke reports why: a handler setting that is not one of those
forms, a missing or unparsable script, a handler function that does not exist, a module that does not
import, or, for a `<script>.ps1::<function>` handler, any terminating error in the script's top-level
code (which runs once, at start-up). The handler can be a function, filter or cmdlet, or an alias to
one of these or to a script file.

The event is converted from JSON before the handler runs. An event with two keys that differ only in
case (for example `id` and `ID`, as in a query string `?id=1&ID=2` passed on by an HTTP front end)
cannot be converted, so the invocation fails before the handler is called.

Leave a handler early with `return`. A `break` or `continue` outside a loop of the handler's own fails
the invocation, or, in the top-level code of a `<script>.ps1::<function>` script or a handler module,
fails start-up. `exit` in a handler function, or a `break` or `continue` whose label matches no loop of
the handler's own, ends the runtime: the invocation fails and Lambda starts the runtime again. In the
top-level code of a `<script>.ps1::<function>` script or a handler module, such a labelled `break` or
`continue` fails start-up, but `exit` stops only that script or module import, and start-up goes on
without the rest of it, so do not use `exit` there. In a `<script>.ps1` handler, `exit` ends only the
script: its exit code is ignored, and what it wrote before is sent as the response.

The Layer and SelfContainedZip targets run pwsh in invariant-globalization mode, because the
`provided.al2023` base has no ICU library, so culture-specific code fails there: a named culture such
as `[cultureinfo]'de-DE'`, or formatting with one, throws. The CMR and Container targets install ICU
and support it.

Lambda reuses the runtime's session for the invocations that follow. After each one, the runtime
removes any jobs (including thread jobs still running) and remoting sessions the invocation left, and
restores `$LASTEXITCODE` and `$env:PSModulePath` to their values at the end of start-up. Anything else
the handler changes, such as a global variable, an environment variable the runtime does not set, the
current location or an imported module, is still there for the next invocation. So are the jobs,
event subscriptions (`-Action`) and remoting sessions that the handler's start-up code created.
